\begin{abstract}
The SIM-QSTATE-ASTRO-v9.1-FINAL-INTEGRATED project, authored by 
C.~Matthew Blackwell, presents a unified architectural blueprint for an 
exascale-capable relativistic simulation engine integrating post-quantum 
cryptography, zero-knowledge proof verification, and GPU-accelerated 
spacetime tensor computation. The system is implemented in modern \texttt{C++20} 
and \texttt{CUDA}, targeting NVIDIA architectures from Ampere through Blackwell, 
and provides hardened multi-stage containerization for secure deployment.

The engine incorporates lattice-based ML-KEM cryptographic key rotation, 
R1CS/zk-SNARK witness validation, and asynchronous double-buffered device 
streams for high-throughput multi-messenger ingestion. CUDA kernels implement 
transverse-traceless gravitational wave tensor projections and Lindblad 
dissipation passes, enabling quantum density matrix evolution under 
relativistic perturbations. Host-side invariance locks enforce strict 
trace-preservation of quantum states, while telemetry buffers support 
Gaia DR3 astrometric vectors and LIGO strain profiles.

Together, these components form a complete, secure, and autonomous 
computational framework for relativistic spacetime simulation, quantum 
dynamics, and cryptographically verified observational data processing. 
The full LaTeX architectural documentation and source code are provided 
to support reproducibility, peer review, and exascale deployment.
\end{abstract}